<SubSection title="Types of Energy" id="Types of Energy">
<p>An open system allows the exchange of an extensive property through bulk material transfer with its surroundings. In an open system, energy is exchanged through movement of material and all mass possesses energy. A closed system allows for the transfer of an extensive property through means other than bulk material transfer. Heat and work are forms of energy that transit across the system boundary in the absence of any physical material.</p>

<CalloutContainer slot="cards">
<CalloutCard title="Remember!">
        <u class="m-0"></u> <p><b>Total energy</b> is conserved while mechanical, electrical, and thermal energy are not conserved. This is because energy is neither created nor destroyed in the universe (except for nuclear reactions) but merely changes form.</p>
</CalloutCard>
</CalloutContainer>
</SubSection>

<SubSubSection title="Potential Energy" id="Potential Energy">
<p>Potential energy (<InlineEquation equation="E_P" />) is the stored energy an object or system has based on its position or arrangement. To consider the change in potential energy of a mass between two different positions or heights:</p> 
<DisplayEquation title="" background="True" equation="\\Delta E_P = E_{P,2}-E_{P,1}= \\dot{m}g (h_2-h_1)" />
<p>Where</p>
<Itemize>
<Item> <InlineEquation equation="E_P" /> = potential energy [<InlineEquation equation="J" />] </Item>
<Item> <InlineEquation equation="\\dot{m}" /> = mass flow rate [<InlineEquation equation="\\frac{kg}{s}" />] </Item>
<Item> g = gravitational acceleration constant [<InlineEquation equation="\\frac{kg}{s^2}" />] </Item>
<Item> h = height relative to a reference plane [<InlineEquation equation="m" />] </Item>
</Itemize>
</SubSubSection>

<SubSubSection title="Kinetic Energy" id="Kinetic Energy">
<p>Kinetic energy (<InlineEquation equation="E_K" />) is the energy an object has because it is in motion. The <InlineEquation equation="E_K" /> of a system is calculated using the mass of a body and the velocity at which it is moving. Since kinetic energy is a scalar quantity, a direction for the velocity does not need to be denoted. Kinetic energy can also move into and out of a system with a flow rate described by <InlineEquation equation="\\dot{m}" />. The rate of kinetic energy is calculated as:</p>
<DisplayEquation title="" background="True" equation="E_K = \\frac{1}{2}\\dot{m}v^2" />
<p>Where</p>
<Itemize>
<Item> <InlineEquation equation="E_K" /> = kinetic energy [<InlineEquation equation="J" />] </Item>
<Item> <InlineEquation equation="\\dot{m}" /> = mass flow rate [<InlineEquation equation="\\frac{kg}{s}" />] </Item>
<Item> v = velocity of object [<InlineEquation equation="\\frac{m}{s}" />] </Item>
</Itemize>
</SubSubSection>

<SubSubSection title="Heat and Work" id="Heat_and_Work">
<p>Heat and work only exist when a system, and hence a system boundary, has been established. Heat and work can only be understood as the movement of energy through direct and non-direct contacts—not bulk material transfer.</p>
<Image src="/cons/heatandwork.png" width="5">
  <span class="fw-bold">System Diagram</span>: A schematic representation of a system illustrating its key features, including system boundaries and depictions of energy flow.
</Image>

<p><b>Heat (<InlineEquation equation="\\dot{Q}" />)</b> is energy that flows due to a difference in temperature. The direction of heat flow is always from a region of higher temperature to one of lower temperature. It is impossible for any system to transfer energy by heat from a cooler to a hotter body.</p>
<Image src="/cons/heatflow.png" width="3">
  <span class="fw-bold">Heat Flow</span>: Schematic illustration of the Clausius Statement of the Second Law.
</Image>
<p>Heat transfer is calculated by:  based on the effective surface area over which the transfer occurs, A, and the temperature gradient: where h is the heat transfer coefficient (per unit area), <InlineEquation equation="T^{surr}" />, is the temperature of the surroundings, and <InlineEquation equation="T^{sys}" /> is the temperature of the system.</p>
<DisplayEquation title="" background="True" equation="\\dot{Q}=hA(T^{surr}-T^{sys})" />
<p>Where</p>
<Itemize>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="\\dot{Q}" /> = heat flow</Item>
<Item>h = heat transfer coefficient </Item>
<Item>A = effective surface area over which the transfer occurs</Item>
<Item>T = temperature</Item>
</Itemize>

<p><b>Work (W)</b> is energy that flows across a system boundary as a result of a driving force other than temperature. Work is a path-dependent function because it requires knowledge on how the final state of the system was reached from the initial state. Work can be done by a fluid flowing into the system. Work can also be added to a system by mixing, stirring, etc. This nonflow work is referred to as shaft work. <InlineEquation equation="\\dot{W}_{shaft} > 0" /> becasue it is added to the system.</p>
<DisplayEquation title="Work Done by Fluid Flow" background="True" equation="\\dot{W}=PAv" />
<p>Where</p>
<Itemize>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="\\dot{W}" /> = work</Item>
<Item>P = pressure </Item>
<Item>A = area</Item>
<Item>v = velocity of fluid</Item>
</Itemize>

</SubSubSection>
<SubSection title="Conservation Statements" id="Conservation Statements">
<p>The total energy of a system is always conserved. The conservation equation for total energy is a mathematical description of the movement and accumulation of total energy in a system of interest. The law of the conservation of total energy states that total energy can be neither created nor destroyed, but only converted from one form into an equivalent quantity of another form of energy.</p>
<p>The generic conservation equation is written to account for the movement of total energy into and out of the system by bulk mass transfer and for the transfer of heat and work across the system boundary. No Generation and Consumption terms are present, since total energy is conserved. The differential form of the conservation of total energy is:</p>
<Image src="/cons/mass&heat&work.png">
  <span class="fw-bold">System Diagram</span>: A schematic representation of a system illustrating its key features, including system boundaries and depictions of energy and mass flows.
</Image>
<DisplayEquation title="Energy Accounting Equation." background="True"  equation="\\sum_{in} \\dot{m}_{in} (\\hat{E}_{P, in}+\\hat{E}_{K, in}+\\hat{H}_{in}) - \\sum_{out} \\dot{m}_{out} (\\hat{E}_{P, out}+\\hat{E}_{K, out}+\\hat{H}_{out})+ \\dot{Q} + \\dot{W}_{shaft} = \\frac{dE^{sys}_T}{dt}" title="" />

     <CalloutCard title="Attention!">
        <u class="m-0"></u>
<p>Note the hat on the energy terms. The hat means that they are intensive because they haven't been multiplied by mass yet. For example, <InlineEquation equation="\\hat{E}_{P, in}=gh_{in}" /> and <InlineEquation equation="\\hat{E}_{K, in} = \\frac{1}{2}v^2" />.</p>
    </CalloutCard>
</SubSection>

<SubSection title="Enthalpy" id="Enthalpy">
<p>Enthalpy is a state function (an intrinsic property) where only the current state matters it is the total sum of heat energy in a system. The path to convert a system from one state to another can be accomplished using the most convenient path.</p>
<Image src="/cons/enthalpy_state.png">
  <span class="fw-bold">Enthalpy state changes</span>: Schematic illustration enthalpy changes.
</Image>
<p>Reference state(s) are arbitrarily chosen phase, temperature, and pressure which we know a specific enthalpy of. Specific enthalpy does not depend on path, so a series of hypothetical steps from one condition to the next that are convenient for calculation can be chosen.</p> 

<DisplayEquation title="Approximating Enthalpy" background="True" equation="\\Delta \\dot{H} = -\\dot{m} (\\hat{H}_{in} - \\hat{H}_{out}) "/>

<Example title="Determining governing equation modifications and boundary conditions." solution="True">
<div class="d-flex flex-row">
<Image src="/cons/Enthalpy.png">
        </Image>
</div>
<div slot="solution">
 <DisplayEquation equation="\\sum_{in} \\dot{m}_{in} (\\hat{E}_{P,in}+\\hat{E}_{K,in}+\\hat{H}_{in}) - \\sum_{out} \\dot{m}_{out} (\\hat{E}_{P,out}+\\hat{E}_{K,out}+\\hat{H}_{out})" />

        <DisplayEquation equation="\\dot{m}_{in} (\\hat{H}_{ref} - \\hat{H}_{in}) + \\dot{m}_{out} (\\hat{H}_{out} - \\hat{H}_{ref})" />

        <DisplayEquation equation="\\dot{m}_{in} (-\\hat{H}_{in}) + \\dot{m}_{out} (\\hat{H}_{out})" />

        <p>
            If <InlineEquation equation="m_{in} = m_{out}" /> ...
        </p>

        <DisplayEquation equation="\\Delta \\dot{H} = -\\dot{m} (\\hat{H}_{in} - \\hat{H}_{out})" />

</div>
</Example>


</SubSection>

<SubSection title="Systems with Chemical Reactions" id="Systems with Chemical Reactions">
<p>During chemical reactions, rearranging the bonds between the atoms of reactants and products causes changes in the internal energy of a system. In reactions, energy is required to break the existing bonds of the reactants, and energy is released during bond formation to create the products. The difference between the final and initial energy states of the products and reactants is known as the heat of reaction.</p>
<p>The heat of reaction or enthalpy of reaction <InlineEquation equation="\\Delta \\hat{H}_r" /> is the enthalpy change for a single process reacting at a specific constant temperature and pressure in which stoichiometric quantities of reactants react completely to form products. The standard heat of reaction <InlineEquation equation="\\Delta \\hat{H}^{\\circ}_r" /> is designated with a degree symbol and is the heat of reaction when both the reactants and products are at a specified reference temperature and pressure, usually 25°C and 1 atm. The heat of reaction is always given on a per-mole basis.</p>

<DisplayEquation title="Energy with Chemical Reactions" background="True" equation="\\Delta \\hat{H}_r = \\sum_{product} (n_{product}\\hat{H}_{product})-\\sum_{reactant} (n_{reactant}\\hat{H}_{reactant})" title="" />

    <CalloutCard title="For Example">
        <u class="m-0"></u>
<p>For a reaction of the format, <InlineEquation equation="aA + bB \\rightarrow pP + qQ" />, the heat of reaction would be <InlineEquation equation="\\Delta \\hat{H}_r = (p\\hat{H}_P)+(q\\hat{H}_Q)-(a\\hat{H}_A)-(b\\hat{H}_B)" />.</p>
    </CalloutCard>
</SubSection>

<SubSection title="Systems with Combustion Reactions" id="Systems with Combustion Reactions">
<p>Heat of Combustion refers to the enthalpy change required to combust 1 mol of an organic substance with oxygen. These are common in many biochemical reactions in the body.</p>
<p>The heat of combustion, <InlineEquation equation="\\Delta \\hat{H}_c" />, is the enthalpy change for a combustion process. Similar to heat of reaction, the standard heat of combustion <InlineEquation equation="\\Delta \\hat{H}^{\\circ}_c" /> is designated with a degree symbol and is the heat of reaction when both the reactants and products are at a specified reference temperature and pressure, 25°C and 1 atm. The heat of combustion is always given on a per-mole basis.</p>

<DisplayEquation title="Heat of Combustion" background="True" equation="\\Delta \\hat{H}_c = \\sum_{reactants} (\\sigma_{reactants}\\hat{H}_{c,reactants})-\\sum_{products} (\\sigma_{products}\\hat{H}_{c,products})" title="" />

    <CalloutCard title="Careful!">
        <u class="m-0"></u>
<p>Note that combustion is reactants minus products! This is important because the heat of combustion values are based on the consumption of the organic compounds, not production</p>
    </CalloutCard>
</SubSection>

<SubSection title="Dynamic Systems" id="Dynamic Systems">
<p>In the case of dynamic systems, the right hand side of the equation will not be zero; instead, it will 'accumulate' internal energy. These cases occur when the system is experiencing energy changes due to mass or temperature changes of the system.</p>
<p>In these cases, the right side of the equation will be converted from change in total energy to internal energy as follows</p>

<DisplayEquation title="Internal Energy" background="True" equation="\\frac{dE^{sys}_T}{dt} = m^{sys} C_v\\frac{dT}{dt}" title="" />

<p>giving an overall equation of</p>

<DisplayEquation title="Dynamic Energy Accounting Equation." background="True"  equation="\\sum_{in} \\dot{m}_{in} (\\hat{E}_{P, in}+\\hat{E}_{K, in}+\\hat{H}_{in}) - \\sum_{in} \\dot{m}_{out} (\\hat{E}_{P, out}+\\hat{E}_{K, out}+\\hat{H}_{out})+ \\dot{Q} + \\dot{W}_{shaft} = m^{sys} C_v\\frac{dT}{dt}" title="" />

